          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p1\",\"quote\":\"Higher resolution observations revealed the tiger stripes to be low ridges with a central fracture.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p3\",\"quote\":\"On average, each tiger stripe depression is 130 kilometers long, 2 kilometers wide, and 500 meters deep.  The flanking ridges are, on average, 100 meters tall and 2–4 kilometers wide.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p3\",\"quote\":\"Given their appearance and their geologic setting within a heavily tectonically deformed region, the tiger stripes are likely to be tectonic fractures.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p5\",\"quote\":\"Nearly the entire surface of Enceladus is covered in a blanket of fine-grained water ice.  The ridges that surround the tiger stripes are often covered in coarse-grained, crystalline water ice.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p5\",\"quote\":\"The Visual and Infrared Mapping Spectrometer (VIMS) instrument also detected trapped carbon dioxide ice and simple organics within the tiger stripes.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p8\",\"quote\":\"Data from the ISS, Ion and Neutral Mass Spectrometer (INMS),  Cosmic Dust Analyser (CDA) and CIRS instruments show that a plume of water vapor and ice, methane , carbon dioxide , and nitrogen emanates from a series of jets located within the tiger stripes.\"}]}"
        }
